WEBVTT

00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:06.240
The AI bubble is about to burst. This is

00:00:03.200 --> 00:00:09.040
what many in Silicon Valley are saying.

00:00:06.240 --> 00:00:12.960
Maybe true, maybe not. But here's what

00:00:09.040 --> 00:00:16.240
is true. Last year, $650

00:00:12.960 --> 00:00:18.480
billion went into AI [music] and 1.1

00:00:16.240 --> 00:00:22.240
million jobs disappeared at the same

00:00:18.480 --> 00:00:25.519
time. I've seen cycles like this before.

00:00:22.240 --> 00:00:27.760
real estate, crypto, and I get to sit in

00:00:25.519 --> 00:00:29.679
boardrooms deciding which AI projects

00:00:27.760 --> 00:00:32.480
get millions [music] in funding and

00:00:29.679 --> 00:00:35.120
which jobs are going to be at stake. The

00:00:32.480 --> 00:00:37.280
story is always the same. The smart

00:00:35.120 --> 00:00:39.840
ones, you know, the [music] top 1%

00:00:37.280 --> 00:00:42.000
position themselves early and win

00:00:39.840 --> 00:00:44.719
whether the crash comes or not, while

00:00:42.000 --> 00:00:46.505
the rest suffer. So, in this video, I

00:00:44.719 --> 00:00:48.719
want to share that exact playbook

00:00:46.505 --> 00:00:51.360
[music] so you can stay ahead, become

00:00:48.719 --> 00:00:53.360
harder to replace, and futureproof your

00:00:51.360 --> 00:00:55.600
career before it's too late. The first

00:00:53.360 --> 00:00:57.760
thing you need to understand, you have

00:00:55.600 --> 00:01:00.079
less time than you think. [music]

00:00:57.760 --> 00:01:02.239
Imagine standing on a beach. The ocean

00:01:00.079 --> 00:01:05.760
suddenly pulls back. You look down at

00:01:02.239 --> 00:01:06.810
your ankles and think, "Well, I'm dry.

00:01:05.760 --> 00:01:06.960
Looks perfectly safe."

00:01:06.810 --> 00:01:09.760
>> [music]

00:01:06.960 --> 00:01:12.960
>> But a thousand miles away, sensors show

00:01:09.760 --> 00:01:15.840
a wall of water racing toward shore at

00:01:12.960 --> 00:01:17.920
500 m an hour. That's what happens in

00:01:15.840 --> 00:01:20.640
every single tsunami. [music] And that

00:01:17.920 --> 00:01:23.520
receding water moment is exactly where

00:01:20.640 --> 00:01:26.159
we are with AI [music] right now. Shane

00:01:23.520 --> 00:01:28.720
Le, co-founder of Google's Deep Mind,

00:01:26.159 --> 00:01:30.320
puts it very bluntly. He says, "If your

00:01:28.720 --> 00:01:32.640
job is 100% [music]

00:01:30.320 --> 00:01:35.600
done on a laptop, you're competing with

00:01:32.640 --> 00:01:37.840
AI that costs pennies." As this AI

00:01:35.600 --> 00:01:40.240
tsunami reaches us, it will create two

00:01:37.840 --> 00:01:42.960
zones for all of us. The high ground

00:01:40.240 --> 00:01:45.200
where the 1% will survive and capture

00:01:42.960 --> 00:01:48.399
millions in wealth and success and the

00:01:45.200 --> 00:01:50.320
flood zone where perhaps 99% of the

00:01:48.399 --> 00:01:52.240
people will end up standing. Which

00:01:50.320 --> 00:01:54.880
brings us to the second thing you need

00:01:52.240 --> 00:01:56.960
to know. How to recognize if you are

00:01:54.880 --> 00:01:59.520
standing in the flood zone right now. I

00:01:56.960 --> 00:02:01.920
was in a meeting watching an AI product

00:01:59.520 --> 00:02:04.000
demo [music] last month. beautifully

00:02:01.920 --> 00:02:06.240
designed, lighting fast responses,

00:02:04.000 --> 00:02:08.800
cutting edge AI at the back end. But

00:02:06.240 --> 00:02:12.239
when I asked, "How many customers use

00:02:08.800 --> 00:02:14.640
this daily?" Silence. The response was,

00:02:12.239 --> 00:02:16.800
"Well, we're not ready for prime time

00:02:14.640 --> 00:02:19.440
yet." But we're working on that right

00:02:16.800 --> 00:02:22.319
now. And I nodded. I smiled, said

00:02:19.440 --> 00:02:24.239
nothing. But the translation, a million

00:02:22.319 --> 00:02:27.120
dollar science project, [music]

00:02:24.239 --> 00:02:31.040
and that's the Flood Zone reality. Great

00:02:27.120 --> 00:02:33.360
demos, zero customers, zero cash. So,

00:02:31.040 --> 00:02:34.959
how do you know if your company is in

00:02:33.360 --> 00:02:38.959
the flood zone? You can apply a

00:02:34.959 --> 00:02:41.440
framework I call rail. R A I L. Just

00:02:38.959 --> 00:02:45.760
four questions, takes about 50 seconds.

00:02:41.440 --> 00:02:47.680
R is revenue. Is your AI used by real

00:02:45.760 --> 00:02:50.080
paying customers? [music] And if the

00:02:47.680 --> 00:02:52.400
answer is, well, we're still piloting or

00:02:50.080 --> 00:02:55.120
eventually we'll get there, that's a red

00:02:52.400 --> 00:02:56.959
flag. If you are leading a project with

00:02:55.120 --> 00:02:58.816
no clear revenue path, you're going to

00:02:56.959 --> 00:03:02.239
be the first one on the chopping block,

00:02:58.816 --> 00:03:04.720
[music] no revenue, no boat. A is

00:03:02.239 --> 00:03:07.599
acceleration. Can you deliver something

00:03:04.720 --> 00:03:10.239
real to customers in 2 weeks? Because if

00:03:07.599 --> 00:03:12.319
you can't, somebody else will for sure.

00:03:10.239 --> 00:03:14.879
So figure out where the friction is and

00:03:12.319 --> 00:03:17.120
how can AI remove [music] it. This is

00:03:14.879 --> 00:03:19.360
your efficiency play. If you can't show

00:03:17.120 --> 00:03:23.052
real improvement in speed and efficiency

00:03:19.360 --> 00:03:25.200
from AI, it's a flood zone. I stands

00:03:23.052 --> 00:03:27.200
[music] for inmarket. So it's possible

00:03:25.200 --> 00:03:28.959
that you might be working on an internal

00:03:27.200 --> 00:03:31.760
tool. Now that's not going to generate

00:03:28.959 --> 00:03:35.280
cash directly, but that's fine. But is

00:03:31.760 --> 00:03:37.920
it in the hands of your real users? Is

00:03:35.280 --> 00:03:39.920
it gathering real data? If it's just

00:03:37.920 --> 00:03:42.319
sitting on a shelf in a test

00:03:39.920 --> 00:03:44.720
environment, it's not a business asset.

00:03:42.319 --> 00:03:46.959
It's a hobby. And finally, L is

00:03:44.720 --> 00:03:49.200
learning. Are you learning from real

00:03:46.959 --> 00:03:52.879
customer use? This fourth one is a

00:03:49.200 --> 00:03:55.440
dealbreaker because one week of watching

00:03:52.879 --> 00:03:58.000
customers break your product teaches you

00:03:55.440 --> 00:04:01.280
more than 6 months of internal testing.

00:03:58.000 --> 00:04:03.760
Also, the pace of AI is so feverish

00:04:01.280 --> 00:04:06.560
right now that if you are not constantly

00:04:03.760 --> 00:04:08.319
learning, you're already [music] losing.

00:04:06.560 --> 00:04:11.519
And I've spoken about companies like

00:04:08.319 --> 00:04:13.519
cursor before. They are an AI based

00:04:11.519 --> 00:04:15.680
product that helps you write code and

00:04:13.519 --> 00:04:18.959
they refine their product [music] on a

00:04:15.680 --> 00:04:21.759
daily basis launching a new feature

00:04:18.959 --> 00:04:24.720
every single day. That is [music] the

00:04:21.759 --> 00:04:27.120
velocity of innovation that we all have

00:04:24.720 --> 00:04:29.919
to get used to. I'm still getting used

00:04:27.120 --> 00:04:32.479
to it. If your answer is no to at least

00:04:29.919 --> 00:04:35.120
two out of those four questions, the

00:04:32.479 --> 00:04:37.600
company you're building or working for

00:04:35.120 --> 00:04:39.688
might be in the flood zone. We have to

00:04:37.600 --> 00:04:41.600
ask the most important question, right?

00:04:39.688 --> 00:04:44.800
[music] How do you get out of the flood

00:04:41.600 --> 00:04:47.440
zone? How do you get to the high ground?

00:04:44.800 --> 00:04:50.080
You get there with the right map. The

00:04:47.440 --> 00:04:52.639
map that 99% of the people will never

00:04:50.080 --> 00:04:54.753
see. The AI world today looks so

00:04:52.639 --> 00:04:55.280
confusing and chaotic from the outside.

00:04:54.753 --> 00:04:58.000
[music]

00:04:55.280 --> 00:05:00.000
Orchestration layer, vector databases,

00:04:58.000 --> 00:05:02.639
tensor chips, things that didn't exist

00:05:00.000 --> 00:05:06.639
10 years ago. But here's what you should

00:05:02.639 --> 00:05:08.960
do. Ignore all that noise. All you need

00:05:06.639 --> 00:05:10.800
is a simple map that shows [music] you

00:05:08.960 --> 00:05:13.093
the three layers where the money is

00:05:10.800 --> 00:05:13.919
made. Layer number one, infrastructure.

00:05:13.093 --> 00:05:15.840
[music]

00:05:13.919 --> 00:05:18.479
And this is where the fabs and chip

00:05:15.840 --> 00:05:22.479
makers and cloud and energy providers

00:05:18.479 --> 00:05:25.360
live. This is a game of massive capital.

00:05:22.479 --> 00:05:28.639
Unless you're Nvidia or have sovereign

00:05:25.360 --> 00:05:31.199
fund backing, it's hard to win here.

00:05:28.639 --> 00:05:35.039
Layer 2 is the frontier models. Chad

00:05:31.199 --> 00:05:37.520
GPT, Claude, Gemini, XAI, and all the

00:05:35.039 --> 00:05:40.880
tooling companies around those models.

00:05:37.520 --> 00:05:42.400
This layer is consolidating really fast.

00:05:40.880 --> 00:05:43.280
Everyone's copying each other's

00:05:42.400 --> 00:05:46.800
features. [music]

00:05:43.280 --> 00:05:49.440
Price per token has dropped by 98% in 18

00:05:46.800 --> 00:05:51.840
months, and also open- source models are

00:05:49.440 --> 00:05:54.240
closing the gap. They're [music] 80% as

00:05:51.840 --> 00:05:56.560
good, but way cheaper. So, think about

00:05:54.240 --> 00:05:58.560
it. You wouldn't start an internet

00:05:56.560 --> 00:06:01.520
search company today, would you? Why

00:05:58.560 --> 00:06:03.759
start a meto model company? Makes no

00:06:01.520 --> 00:06:05.440
sense. Now think about the dotcom era.

00:06:03.759 --> 00:06:08.240
Remember the companies that [music] laid

00:06:05.440 --> 00:06:10.479
fiber optic cables? Most of them went

00:06:08.240 --> 00:06:13.120
bankrupt. The ones who build apps on top

00:06:10.479 --> 00:06:16.720
of them like Amazon, YouTube, [music]

00:06:13.120 --> 00:06:19.600
PayPal, they won. And that brings us to

00:06:16.720 --> 00:06:22.400
layer three, which is about apps and

00:06:19.600 --> 00:06:23.280
services. This is the interface, the

00:06:22.400 --> 00:06:25.759
solution. [music]

00:06:23.280 --> 00:06:27.600
This layer is where the value is

00:06:25.759 --> 00:06:30.240
captured. If you're starting something

00:06:27.600 --> 00:06:32.160
on your own, this is where you should

00:06:30.240 --> 00:06:34.400
focus. Now, there are three ways to

00:06:32.160 --> 00:06:38.000
think about this layer. Horizontal AI

00:06:34.400 --> 00:06:40.880
apps, vertical AI apps, and AI services.

00:06:38.000 --> 00:06:43.039
Horizontal AI apps work for everyone.

00:06:40.880 --> 00:06:46.639
So, think of apps like Gamma for

00:06:43.039 --> 00:06:49.440
presentation or perplexity for AI based

00:06:46.639 --> 00:06:51.680
search. Huge market, but it's a

00:06:49.440 --> 00:06:54.000
distribution battle. You need millions

00:06:51.680 --> 00:06:56.319
of users to become profitable. Vertical

00:06:54.000 --> 00:06:59.039
apps on the other hand go deep. Think

00:06:56.319 --> 00:07:01.599
like cursor for developers or open

00:06:59.039 --> 00:07:03.599
evidence for medicine. Smaller market

00:07:01.599 --> 00:07:06.080
but you own your niche. [music] And

00:07:03.599 --> 00:07:08.160
finally AI services and they're the

00:07:06.080 --> 00:07:11.120
implementation layer. Companies like

00:07:08.160 --> 00:07:14.160
scale AI has proven that humans in the

00:07:11.120 --> 00:07:16.560
loop is a massive business opportunity

00:07:14.160 --> 00:07:19.440
because [music] they handle the messy

00:07:16.560 --> 00:07:22.319
data work that models can't do yet. The

00:07:19.440 --> 00:07:24.319
second big services opportunity here is

00:07:22.319 --> 00:07:27.039
integration. [music] And I know boutique

00:07:24.319 --> 00:07:28.319
firms right now that charge $50,000 a

00:07:27.039 --> 00:07:30.960
month to [music] help traditional

00:07:28.319 --> 00:07:32.880
companies plug AI into their workflows.

00:07:30.960 --> 00:07:34.400
And they're not building software.

00:07:32.880 --> 00:07:36.720
They're just implementing it. They're

00:07:34.400 --> 00:07:39.360
the experts [music] who charge you to

00:07:36.720 --> 00:07:41.280
set it up. So that's the map. Layer 1

00:07:39.360 --> 00:07:43.759
and two are where the giants are

00:07:41.280 --> 00:07:45.759
fighting. Layer three is where you can

00:07:43.759 --> 00:07:48.479
build something amazing. Here's what

00:07:45.759 --> 00:07:50.800
most people miss. Knowing the map isn't

00:07:48.479 --> 00:07:53.120
enough, right? You need to [music] know

00:07:50.800 --> 00:07:55.520
how to avoid the potholes when you're

00:07:53.120 --> 00:07:58.000
moving to the high ground. That's why

00:07:55.520 --> 00:08:00.080
number four is about avoiding the

00:07:58.000 --> 00:08:04.080
potholes when you get to the high

00:08:00.080 --> 00:08:06.670
ground. The job apocalypse from AI may

00:08:04.080 --> 00:08:09.039
be inevitable, but it's not here yet.

00:08:06.670 --> 00:08:11.520
[music] Sure, hiring has slowed down,

00:08:09.039 --> 00:08:14.240
but companies are still hiring at a

00:08:11.520 --> 00:08:16.319
slower pace. And the companies that are

00:08:14.240 --> 00:08:19.115
laying off people first are also the

00:08:16.319 --> 00:08:20.080
earliest adopters of AI. Microsoft,

00:08:19.115 --> 00:08:24.080
[music]

00:08:20.080 --> 00:08:27.280
Amazon, Meta, Google, others, and

00:08:24.080 --> 00:08:30.400
they're cutting nonAI roles to fund AI

00:08:27.280 --> 00:08:32.719
talent. So in this world, if your job is

00:08:30.400 --> 00:08:34.800
to maintain the status quo, you are

00:08:32.719 --> 00:08:36.880
unfortunately in the flood zone. So to

00:08:34.800 --> 00:08:40.159
get to the high ground, you have to

00:08:36.880 --> 00:08:43.839
move. AI will automate the scripted

00:08:40.159 --> 00:08:46.320
parts of any job. Your survival depends

00:08:43.839 --> 00:08:47.920
on owning the [music] strategic parts.

00:08:46.320 --> 00:08:50.320
It doesn't matter if you're a software

00:08:47.920 --> 00:08:53.760
engineer or a consultant or [music] a

00:08:50.320 --> 00:08:56.560
data annotator or accountant or analyst.

00:08:53.760 --> 00:08:59.680
The pattern is going to be the same.

00:08:56.560 --> 00:09:02.080
Scripted work gets automated. Strategic

00:08:59.680 --> 00:09:04.311
judgment stays human. Here's your

00:09:02.080 --> 00:09:06.399
three-step action plan. First step,

00:09:04.311 --> 00:09:09.200
[music] audit your job. How much of your

00:09:06.399 --> 00:09:11.040
day is scripted versus strategic?

00:09:09.200 --> 00:09:13.760
Scripted work is [music] like the

00:09:11.040 --> 00:09:16.720
mind-numbing repetitive activities.

00:09:13.760 --> 00:09:19.680
processing data, summarizing meetings,

00:09:16.720 --> 00:09:22.480
drafting standard internal emails,

00:09:19.680 --> 00:09:25.440
writing boilerplate code, whatever.

00:09:22.480 --> 00:09:27.760
Strategic work is about solving problems

00:09:25.440 --> 00:09:29.519
no one has seen before, building client

00:09:27.760 --> 00:09:32.640
relationships, designing system [music]

00:09:29.519 --> 00:09:34.480
architecture, catching edge cases that

00:09:32.640 --> 00:09:37.279
AI sometimes misses. [music]

00:09:34.480 --> 00:09:40.320
So, audit your work and divide it into

00:09:37.279 --> 00:09:41.120
these two categories, scripted and

00:09:40.320 --> 00:09:44.080
strategic. [music]

00:09:41.120 --> 00:09:46.480
Step number two, fire yourself from the

00:09:44.080 --> 00:09:48.399
scripted parts. Automate all of it

00:09:46.480 --> 00:09:50.399
before your boss does it for you. And

00:09:48.399 --> 00:09:52.959
step number three, reinvest [music]

00:09:50.399 --> 00:09:55.279
that time in developing your human edge

00:09:52.959 --> 00:09:58.560
on the strategic side. Now, this sounds

00:09:55.279 --> 00:10:01.839
counterintuitive somehow, but I work

00:09:58.560 --> 00:10:04.580
less on execution now and more on

00:10:01.839 --> 00:10:05.680
daydreaming. I actively disconnect,

00:10:04.580 --> 00:10:08.720
[music]

00:10:05.680 --> 00:10:11.279
stare out the window, take long walks,

00:10:08.720 --> 00:10:14.640
and from the outside, it may not look

00:10:11.279 --> 00:10:16.959
like work at all, but this is deep work

00:10:14.640 --> 00:10:19.839
because deep [music] thinking requires

00:10:16.959 --> 00:10:22.640
silence, not scrolling. It requires

00:10:19.839 --> 00:10:24.720
imagination, not information. [music]

00:10:22.640 --> 00:10:27.040
That is where you can connect the dots

00:10:24.720 --> 00:10:29.462
that others cannot see. Cultivating

00:10:27.040 --> 00:10:31.360
these right habits alone won't save

00:10:29.462 --> 00:10:34.000
[music] you. Not without the right

00:10:31.360 --> 00:10:36.000
mindset. The final framework you should

00:10:34.000 --> 00:10:38.959
know is the three Rs. You know, the top

00:10:36.000 --> 00:10:41.360
1% who will make it to the high ground

00:10:38.959 --> 00:10:43.872
will all share three things in common.

00:10:41.360 --> 00:10:45.839
The three Rs, rigor, relationship,

00:10:43.872 --> 00:10:48.079
[music] and resilience. There are

00:10:45.839 --> 00:10:49.600
hundreds of AI startups in healthcare.

00:10:48.079 --> 00:10:52.959
They have [music] come and gone. But

00:10:49.600 --> 00:10:55.760
open evidence is worth $3.5 billion

00:10:52.959 --> 00:11:00.240
right now, and it's used by 40% of US

00:10:55.760 --> 00:11:03.920
doctors daily. Why rigor? They

00:11:00.240 --> 00:11:05.920
fine-tuned their AI with such meticulous

00:11:03.920 --> 00:11:07.920
peer-reviewed research and human

00:11:05.920 --> 00:11:10.800
feedback. Doctors just [music] love

00:11:07.920 --> 00:11:12.800
them. They evaluated every edge case

00:11:10.800 --> 00:11:15.680
that would break most other startups.

00:11:12.800 --> 00:11:18.399
They understood their domain and the

00:11:15.680 --> 00:11:21.680
rigor it takes to succeed in it. And the

00:11:18.399 --> 00:11:24.720
data backs this up. BCG and Harvard ran

00:11:21.680 --> 00:11:26.880
a study with consultants using GPT4.

00:11:24.720 --> 00:11:30.720
Those with a deep understanding of their

00:11:26.880 --> 00:11:34.240
domain got 40% better results. Those

00:11:30.720 --> 00:11:36.480
without only 12%. So that's the first

00:11:34.240 --> 00:11:39.519
thing you need to stay on high ground.

00:11:36.480 --> 00:11:41.600
Rigor, deep understanding of your

00:11:39.519 --> 00:11:44.640
domain, your process, [music]

00:11:41.600 --> 00:11:48.240
your data, not surface level knowledge,

00:11:44.640 --> 00:11:51.600
but deep mastery of what you do. Second

00:11:48.240 --> 00:11:54.000
are relationships. I learned this simple

00:11:51.600 --> 00:11:56.480
truth while working at a company in New

00:11:54.000 --> 00:12:00.160
York about a decade ago. It was long

00:11:56.480 --> 00:12:01.920
hours, brutal pace. So I was there till

00:12:00.160 --> 00:12:04.079
late night most of the time and I would

00:12:01.920 --> 00:12:05.920
always keep my office door open in the

00:12:04.079 --> 00:12:07.680
evening. My colleagues would stop by and

00:12:05.920 --> 00:12:09.920
we would have a chat. One of my

00:12:07.680 --> 00:12:11.839
colleagues would often stop by and we

00:12:09.920 --> 00:12:14.560
would talk for a while about lots of

00:12:11.839 --> 00:12:18.000
things about his career, his fears, what

00:12:14.560 --> 00:12:21.279
he really wanted to do. And years later,

00:12:18.000 --> 00:12:23.920
he became the CEO of a $150 million

00:12:21.279 --> 00:12:26.959
company. And he told me that those

00:12:23.920 --> 00:12:28.673
conversations changed his mindset and

00:12:26.959 --> 00:12:32.399
trajectory. Not because I said something

